<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nafpaktos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Naupaktos, Naupactus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nafpaktos, ancient Naupaktos and Venetian Lepanto, is a port town on the northern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, guarded by a Venetian castle and a walled harbour. It served as an Athenian naval base in the Peloponnesian War, was sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1407 and taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1499. The naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> of 1571 took its name from the town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nafpaktos.jpg" alt="Nafpaktos" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agnieszka_R · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nafpaktos_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nafpaktos lies on a bay on the north coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a>, 3 km west of the mouth of the river Mornos. It is 9 km north-east of Antirrio and 18 km north-east of Patras. The ancient name Naupaktos means boatyard, from the Greek for ship and for to fix. Venetians called it Lepanto, and the Ottomans İnebahtı.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Greek legend the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracleidae">Heraclidae</a> built the fleet here with which they invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. In antiquity it was a town of the Locri Ozolae with the best harbour on the northern coast of the gulf. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a> it fell to the Athenians, who settled there the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messenians">Messenians</a> forced out after the Third Messenian War in 464 BCE. It was the Athenian headquarters in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> and the scene of the Battle of Naupactus in 429 BCE. Philip II of Macedon gave it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a>, who defended it against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> for two months in 191 BCE. An earthquake destroyed it in 551/2.</p><p>From the late 9th century it was capital of the Byzantine thema of Nicopolis, and a harbour of the Byzantine navy. In 1165 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-of-tudela">Benjamin of Tudela</a> found a Jewish community of about 100 there. After the Fourth Crusade it became part of the Despotate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, and in 1294 it was ceded to Philip I, Prince of Taranto, as part of the dowry of Thamar Angelina Komnene. It passed to the Catalans of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 1361 and to the Albanian despot John Bua Spata in 1376 or 1377. Paul Spata sold it to the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1407.</p><p>After 1449 it was an isolated Venetian exclave in Ottoman territory, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> built strong fortifications. It fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1499 during the Second Ottoman-Venetian War. In 1521 the town had 509 Christian, 84 Jewish and 28 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a> households. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> was fought at the mouth of the gulf on 7 October 1571, when Spanish, Papal and Venetian forces nearly destroyed Ottoman naval power; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-de-cervantes">Miguel de Cervantes</a> fought in it. Venice held the town again from 1687 to 1699, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> returned it to the Ottomans. It became part of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in March 1829.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Nafpaktos had about 19,768 people at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Its main attraction is the port and the castle, both with Venetian remains, and the port has monuments to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> and a statue of Cervantes by the Mallorcan artist Jaume Mir. The Fethiye Mosque is the town's largest Ottoman-era mosque.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nafpaktos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Spanish rule Sicily was a bulwark against the Ottomans, supplying galleys and grain for the relief of Malta in 1565 and the <b>Lepanto</b> campaign of 1571.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Barbary states and a frontier bastion of the Ottoman Empire in the western Mediterranean; its defeat of Charles V in 1541 and its campaigns against Spanish outposts halted Iberian expansion in the central Maghreb, while its ships reinforced the Ottoman navy at <b>Lepanto</b> and in other campaigns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Spanish sea power was shown at <b>Lepanto</b> (1571), where Spanish galleys formed a major part of the Holy League fleet that defeated the Ottomans, but the Armada sent against England in 1588 was defeated by English ships and wrecked by storms on the voyage home round Scotland and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Gjin Bua Shpata took Angelokastron and <b>Lepanto</b> (Naupaktos) in Aetolia, while Peter Losha held the Despotate of Arta.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-angelokastron-and-lepanto">Despotate of Angelokastron and Lepanto</a></li><li><blockquote>An early 5th century BCE bronze tablet records the terms for Locrian colonists at <b>Naupactus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/opuntian-locris">Opuntian Locris</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Naupactus</b> was theirs until Athens gave it to Messenian refugees c. 456 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/ozolian-locris">Ozolian Locris</a></li></ul></section>
